The two-pointer solution, executed:

```go
func isPalindrome(s string) bool {
    for i, j := 0, len(s)-1; i < j; i, j = i+1, j-1 {
        if s[i] != s[j] {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}
// isPalindrome("racecar") → true — verified
// isPalindrome("hello") → false — verified
```

The mechanics: i from the start, j from the end, moving toward each other. Each step compares the pair. The first mismatch returns false immediately — no need to check further. The loop ends (i >= j) on a confirmed palindrome.

The invariants: everything before i and after j is already confirmed; s[i..j] is the unchecked remainder. Each iteration either finds a mismatch (done) or shrinks the range by 2.

The variants that upgrade the question:
- Case-insensitive: strings.ToLower both sides first
- Ignoring non-alphanumerics (the "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama" case): skip or filter
- Multi-word/phrase palindromes: normalize, strip punctuation, then the same two-pointer

The complexity: O(n) time (n/2 comparisons), O(1) space (two indices — no copy, no allocation). The alternative — reverse and compare — is O(n) space for the reversed copy; two-pointer is strictly better.
